  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1239 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nA low pressure system over the FL/GA coast this evening will be \ntracking north through the day. Radar shows light precip over \nwestern NC that is spreading NW, with a few surface obs showing \nlight rain at the surface. We are seeing some downslope winds with \n850 mb easterly winds around 20-30 kt across the mountains. This may \nsomewhat reduce the precip that is able to reach the TN Valley. \nThrough the morning, locations east of I-81 and I-75 could see a \ntrace to a tenth of an inch of rain. The afternoon should be dry as \nthe system moves away.\n\nA shortwave trough will approach from the west Sunday night, with \nthe associated cold front passing through on Monday. Moisture will \nbe quite limited with this system, but some light rain will again be \npossible during the day, from a trace to a few hundredths, mainly in \nthe northern half of the area. Behind the front on Monday afternoon, \na NW flow develops that will keep some rain chances in the East TN \nmountains until the evening. Cooler temperatures follow the front on \nTuesday, with highs in the 50s. \n\nBy the mid-week, a trough is expected to swing across the Deep \nSouth, bringing a return of rain chances. Still some timing and \namplitude uncertainty this far out, but overall, this system looks \nto bring better chances for rain, and perhaps some decent rain \namounts. Depending on the amplification of this system and track of \nthe surface low , we could potentially see some breezy winds across \nthe mountains and foothills. Current timing for rain chances with \nthis system appear to be Wednesday and Wednesday night, perhaps \nlingering into Thanksgiving Day. Dry conditions and near normal \ntemperatures can be expected behind this system for the end of the \nweek.",
